    {
      "id": "bqtm6tz",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.4 Major Rivers of Karnataka"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Karnataka is the source of several peninsular rivers. Most rivers originate in the Western Ghats and flow eastward into the Bay of Bengal; only a few flow west into the Arabian Sea.",
      "id": "tk8jdh3"
    },
    {
      "id": "q8b0rjx",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>River</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Origin</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Direction</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Flows Into</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Important Facts</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Cauvery (Kaveri)</p>",
            "<p>Talakaveri, Brahmagiri Hills, Kodagu</p>",
            "<p>East</p>",
            "<p>Bay of Bengal</p>",
            "<p>Lifeline of South Karnataka; Kaveri Water Dispute (Karnataka-Tamil Nadu); KRS Dam, Harangi, Hemavathi, Kabini are tributaries/dams</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Krishna</p>",
            "<p>Mahabaleshwar, Maharashtra</p>",
            "<p>East</p>",
            "<p>Bay of Bengal</p>",
            "<p>Enters Karnataka at Bijapur (Vijayapura); Almatti &amp; Narayanpur Dams; Malaprabha &amp; Ghataprabha are tributaries</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Tungabhadra</p>",
            "<p>Merger of Tunga &amp; Bhadra rivers at Kudli, Shivamogga</p>",
            "<p>East</p>",
            "<p>Krishna</p>",
            "<p>Major tributary of Krishna; Tungabhadra Dam at Hospete (1953)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Sharavathi</p>",
            "<p>Ambutirtha, Tirthahalli, Shivamogga</p>",
            "<p>West</p>",
            "<p>Arabian Sea</p>",
            "<p>Jog Falls (253 m — highest plunge waterfall in India) on this river; Linganamakki Dam</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Malaprabha</p>",
            "<p>Kanakumbi hills, Khanapur, Belagavi</p>",
            "<p>East</p>",
            "<p>Krishna</p>",
            "<p>Renuka Sagar Reservoir; Navilu Tirtha; Nargund area</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Ghataprabha</p>",
            "<p>Western Ghats near Belagavi</p>",
            "<p>East</p>",
            "<p>Krishna</p>",
            "<p>Hidkal Dam; Gokak Falls on this river (52 m)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Netravathi</p>",
            "<p>Brahmagiri Hills, Kodagu</p>",
            "<p>West</p>",
            "<p>Arabian Sea</p>",
            "<p>Passes through Mangaluru; Bantwal Dam; drains D. Kannada</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bedti (Gangavali)</p>",
            "<p>Dharwad plateau</p>",
            "<p>West</p>",
            "<p>Arabian Sea</p>",
            "<p>Magod Falls (198 m) on this river; passes through Uttara Kannada</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kabini</p>",
            "<p>Wayanad plateau, Kerala</p>",
            "<p>North-East</p>",
            "<p>Cauvery</p>",
            "<p>Kabini Reservoir (Mysuru-Kodagu border); excellent wildlife area</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Hemavathi</p>",
            "<p>Bisle Ghat, Sakleshpur</p>",
            "<p>East</p>",
            "<p>Cauvery</p>",
            "<p>Hemavathi Reservoir (Hassan); feeds Cauvery basin</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Arkavathi</p>",
            "<p>Nandi Hills, Chikkaballapur</p>",
            "<p>South</p>",
            "<p>Cauvery</p>",
            "<p>Passes through Bengaluru rural; Manchanabele Dam</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Bhadra</p>",
            "<p>Gangamoola, Chikkamagaluru</p>",
            "<p>East (joins Tunga)</p>",
            "<p>Krishna (via Tungabhadra)</p>",
            "<p>Bhadra Wildlife Sanctuary along its banks; Bhadra Reservoir</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "jhzrwfh",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><img alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/bg9f2zbva2tMPuV_TCmJuTRkGr21rJ3QCIdo7ybT_4b6uCKbdkvdek-5UAf6-8iB1PHiOZiBWqsNMLxbOblxYI9TkKgiNNB3HuRlGpK7xwEBmEtCbwwi4IOU9KhWkI5trkaEyj3vBAu_W_RcPyTdFWni1zFZpEHZTo0WvgrC0cImmU_KbXxU-yKuV4Cmit04?purpose=fullsize\" src=\"/books/Guide/Karnataka_CONSTABLE/images/image_p1mpd5a.jpeg\"/><strong>EXAM NOTE — Waterfalls:</strong></p><p>• Jog Falls (Gerusoppa Falls) — 253 m — Sharavathi River — Shivamogga (tallest plunge waterfall in India)  </p><p>• Gokak Falls — 52 m — Ghataprabha River — Belagavi district</p><p>• Magod Falls — 198 m — Bedti River — Uttara Kannada</p><p>• Abbey Falls — Kodagu (Cauvery tributary)</p><p>• Shivanasamudra Falls — Cauvery River — Mandya; India's first hydroelectric power station (1902)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p982xx1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.5 Mountains, Peaks & Ghats"
    },
    {
      "id": "w61hwyb",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Peak / Range</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>District</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Elevation (m)</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Features</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Mullayanagiri</p>",
            "<p>Chikkamagaluru</p>",
            "<p>1,930 m</p>",
            "<p>Highest peak in Karnataka; part of Baba Budangiri range; popular trekking destination</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Kudremukh</p>",
            "<p>Chikkamagaluru</p>",
            "<p>1,892 m</p>",
            "<p>2nd highest; horseshoe-shaped peak (name = 'horse face'); UNESCO Biosphere Reserve; source of Bhadra &amp; Netravathi</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Baba Budangiri</p>",
            "<p>Chikkamagaluru</p>",
            "<p>1,895 m</p>",
            "<p>Coffee first cultivated in India here (Baba Budan brought 7 seeds from Mecca ~1600 CE); sacred to both Hindus &amp; Muslims</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Brahmagiri</p>",
            "<p>Kodagu</p>",
            "<p>1,608 m</p>",
            "<p>Source of River Cauvery (Talakaveri shrine)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Pushpagiri</p>",
            "<p>Kodagu-Chikkamagaluru border</p>",
            "<p>1,714 m</p>",
            "<p>Part of Western Ghats UNESCO WH site; Pushpagiri Wildlife Sanctuary</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Tadiyandamol</p>",
            "<p>Kodagu</p>",
            "<p>1,748 m</p>",
            "<p>Highest peak in Kodagu; accessible trek from Kakkabe</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Nandi Hills (Nandidurga)</p>",
            "<p>Chikkaballapur</p>",
            "<p>1,478 m</p>",
            "<p>Source of Arkavathi &amp; Palar rivers; Tipu Sultan's summer retreat; near Bengaluru</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Savandurga</p>",
            "<p>Ramanagara</p>",
            "<p>1,226 m</p>",
            "<p>One of Asia's largest monolithic rocks; rocky hill</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Skandagiri (Kalavara Durga)</p>",
            "<p>Chikkaballapur</p>",
            "<p>1,450 m</p>",
            "<p>Cloud trekking destination; fort ruins</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Devaraibetta</p>",
            "<p>Chikkaballapur</p>",
            "<p>1,501 m</p>",
            "<p>Highest point in Nandi range</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1gt8o4d",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1.6 Climate of Karnataka"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Karnataka has a tropical monsoon climate. The state experiences four distinct seasons:",
      "id": "7rulfn3"
    },
    {
      "id": "ehrojz8",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Season</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Period</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Temperature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Key Features</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Winter (Cold Season)</p>",
            "<p>November – February</p>",
            "<p>15°C – 25°C</p>",
            "<p>Mild cold in plateau; cool nights in Malnad; light NE monsoon rains in coastal areas</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Summer (Hot Season)</p>",
            "<p>March – May</p>",
            "<p>28°C – 42°C</p>",
            "<p>Very hot in north Karnataka; Bidar, Raichur, Kalaburagi can exceed 42°C; pre-monsoon thunderstorms</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>South-West Monsoon</p>",
            "<p>June – September</p>",
            "<p>20°C – 30°C</p>",
            "<p>Main rainfall season; Coastal &amp; Malnad get 2,500–3,800 mm; plateau gets 600–900 mm; NE Karnataka least rainfall</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Retreating Monsoon</p>",
            "<p>October – November</p>",
            "<p>20°C – 28°C</p>",
            "<p>NE monsoon brings rain to SE Karnataka &amp; Tamil Nadu</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "7fevd3j",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Highest Rainfall District</strong></p>",
            "<p>Dakshina Kannada &amp; Uttara Kannada (coastal); Agumbe in Shivamogga gets ~7,000 mm (2nd rainiest in India)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Lowest Rainfall District</strong></p>",
            "<p>Bidar, Kalaburagi (less than 500 mm annually)</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Agumbe</strong></p>",
            "<p>Called the 'Cherrapunji of South India'; in Malnad region, Shivamogga</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Soil Types</strong></p>",
            "<p>Red laterite (south), Black cotton (north-central Deccan), Alluvial (coastal), Sandy (coastal strip)</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "enriched": false
}